<p id="p1">In <a href="https://www.macstories.net/notes/macos-27-3s-full-disk-access-changes-no-biggie/" rel="noopener noreferrer">another</a> support document shared yesterday, Apple has begun sharing more details on how <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/127901" rel="noopener noreferrer">Siri AI will be capped by daily usage limits</a> in 2027:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote2"><p>
  Starting with the 2027 software releases, Siri AI and Apple Intelligence features are available for free for users with a supported device set to a supported language. Some features rely on powerful server models and have limits where increased access is available with eligible iCloud+ plans, starting with the 27.3 release. These include, but aren’t limited to, features in these categories:</p>
<ul><li>Siri AI</li>
<li>Images: Intelligent Photo Editing Tools including Clean Up, Extend, and Spatial Reframing, and Image Playground</li>
<li>Workflows and automation: AFM Cloud and Cloud Pro models in Shortcuts</li>
<li>Third-party apps using Apple Foundation Models in Private Cloud Compute</li>
</ul></blockquote>
<p id="p3">And:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote4"><p>
  Siri AI in the 2027 software releases is more capable, conversational, and personal, so you can get more done.</p>
<p>  Siri AI includes a generous daily usage limit. If you reach it, you will be notified, and you can still make everyday Siri requests like “Set a timer for 3 minutes” or “Text Mark that Annika’s party is at 123 Main Street.”</p>
<p>  If you reach your limit, advanced requests are paused until your limit resets or you upgrade to an eligible iCloud+ plan. Advanced requests are more complex, like “Text Mark the address for Annika’s party, and tell him what time we need to leave from home to make it on time” or “Add a summary of my San Francisco trip note to this email draft.”
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p5">I’m not surprised that Apple’s following the rest of the AI industry in applying usage limits to cloud-hosted models.</p>
<p id="p6">I do think it’s a little funny that we now have to worry about limits for models that are, to put it bluntly, quite bad compared to literally <a href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/#intelligence" rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>anything else</em></a> at the frontier of AI right now. It’s even funnier to imagine a timeline where people will be begging John Ternus for Siri AI resets like they do with <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thibault_Sottiaux" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenAI’s Tibo</a>.</p>
